      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introducttion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>Introduction</h3>After using marks to count, people created new ideas to make counting better. This time, they made shapes (symbols) to show our fingers. In this text, we look at counting ideas made by Indigenous people.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_drawing",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>Basic shapes</h3>Let's remember one of the first ways to count, shown in <b>Figure 1</b> (<htcite0> ; <htcite1>):</p><p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgBone\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgBone', '0%')\"  class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: Representation of baboon bone adorned with markings. This image, credited to Enrique Silván Pobes, is featured in the scholarly work of Francisco A. Gonzáles Redondo et al.<br />The original is available in ResearchNet (<htcite2>), and its inclusion here is made possible by the authorization of Doctor Francisco A. Gonzales Redondo.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a4f22e25-5839-407f-a1aa-634521d52b42",
                     "text": "González Redondo, Francisco A., et al.",
                     "page": "pp. 174 - 177, 180 - 182",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2010",
                        "month": "07",
                        "day": "25"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ceffba8a-43be-4de3-a5c8-27cdf81311b1",
                     "text": "Puente, Francisco Barriga",
                     "page": "pp. 21 - 23",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2020",
                        "month": "07",
                        "day": "25"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "adaed4fb-9c50-4ef9-ab79-c65f3c0629b8",
                     "text": "González Redondo, Francisco A., et al.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2010",
                        "month": "12",
                        "day": "25"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2010",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2020",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2010",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p>Bones and tools are not common to have near us. But we can make shapes (drawings) to show our fingers. A shape that can help us with this is the rectangle:</p><p><div class=\"htTwoGeometricalRectangle\"> <div class=\"htFirstGeometrical\"><div class=\"htVerticalRectangle\"></div></div> <div class=\"htSecondGeometrical\"><div class=\"htHorizontalRectangle\"></div></div> </div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 2</b>: Demonstration of rectangles with different orientations: vertical (pink) and horizontal (blue).</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p>Nature follows a rule: big things are made of smaller parts. For example, a rectangle can be made from a smaller shape, the square:</p><p><div class=\"htTwoGeometricalRectangle\"> <div class=\"htFirstGeometrical\"><div class=\"htHorizontalRectangle\"></div></div> <div class=\"htSecondGeometrical\"><div class=\"htSquare htColorSoftRed \"></div><div class=\"htSquare htColorSoftYellow\"></div></div> </div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 3</b>: Demonstration of a rectangle on the left, and on the right, the same rectangle made from squares of different colors.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Reducing...</b><br />We have used squares to make a rectangle. Can we also use even smaller squares to make other shapes?</div></div></center></p><p>Yes, we can. For example, we could use smaller squares to form the squares we saw.</p><p><h4>Besides the square</h4>Another closed shape, taken from nature, is the circle. We can see it in our eyes:</p><p><div class=\"htTwoGeometricalRectangle\"> <div class=\"htFirstGeometrical\"><div class=\"htEye\"></div></div> <div class=\"htSecondGeometrical\"><div class=\"htCircle\"></div></div> </div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 4</b>: On the left, we have a representation of an eye. On the right, we have a circle. Eye inspired by the source code available on <htcite0>.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "35093614-5991-4b27-863a-5d2bf4d35905",
                     "text": "CodePen",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>Practice</h3></p><p class=\"lab\"><b>Practice</b><br />Draw the shapes learned in the space below and color the eye in <b>Figure 4</b> with the color you prefer:</p><p><table class=\"draw\"><tr><td>Circle</td><td>Square</td><td>Rectangle</td></tr><tr><td><img src=\"images/HistoryTracers/White.png\" class=\"imgcenter\"/></td><td><img src=\"images/HistoryTracers/White.png\" class=\"imgcenter\"/></td><td><img src=\"images/HistoryTracers/White.png\" class=\"imgcenter\"/></td></tr></table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 1</b>: Space to draw the studied shapes.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_andes",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>Our Fingers as Circles</h3>We explore the method <i>\"Yupana Inka Tawa Pukllay\"</i>, made and published by Dhavit Prem of the Associação Yupanki (<htcite0>; <htcite1>). This method uses the vertical model of the Yupana, as shown by Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala (<htcite2>).</p><p>When we look at the <i>Yupana</i>, we can link the circles on it to fingers. Use the arrows on the right side of the <i>Yupana</i> to see how our fingers are shown:</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "2d2ea4a5-506b-4dbf-aaa4-3a10a8fbbd9c",
                     "text": "Prem, Dhavit",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2018",
                        "month": "07",
                        "day": "25"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a62b520a-f443-4d83-ab51-b99c9a46dab8",
                     "text": "Prem, Dhavit",
                     "page": "pp. 354 - 363",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2018",
                        "month": "04",
                        "day": "01"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "f5b74f18-0bc4-46d2-8a5c-64dd80b74dd1",
                     "text": "Poma de Ayala, Felipe Guaman",
                     "page": "pp. 361 - 362",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1615",
                        "month": "02",
                        "day": "13"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2018",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "1615",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"yupana0\" class=\"tawapukllay\"><tr><th colspan=\"4\"><i>Yupana</i></th><th>Left Hand</th><th>Right Hand</th><th>Arrows</th></tr> <tr id=\"tf1\"><td id=\"tc1f1\"><span class=\"dot five_dot_c1_up\"></span><span class=\"dot five_dot_c1_center\"></span><span class=\"dot five_dot_c1_bottom\"></span><span class=\"dot five_dot_c2_up\"></span><span class=\"dot five_dot_c2_bottom\"></span></td> <td id=\"tc2f1\"><span class=\"dot three_dot_bottom\"></span><span class=\"dot three_dot_up\"></span><span class=\"dot three_dot_center\"></span></td> <td id=\"tc3f1\"><span class=\"dot two_dot_bottom\"></span> <span class=\"dot two_dot_up\"></span></td> <td id=\"tc4f1\"><span class=\"dot dot_center\"></span></td><td id=\"tc5f1\"><img onclick=\"htImageZoom('leftHandImg', '0%')\" id=\"leftHandImg\"  /></td><td id=\"tc6f1\"><img id=\"rightHandImg\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('rightHandImg', '0%')\"  /></td><td id=\"tc7f1\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-caret-up upArrowWithFA\" id=\"traineeUp0\"></i>  <i class=\"fa-solid fa-caret-down downArrowWithFA\" id=\"traineeDown0\"></td></tr></table></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 2</b>: Representation of a <i>Yupana</i> line according to Poma de Ayala (<htcite0> ; <htcite1>). CSS inspired by source code available on W3Schools (<htcite2>).</p><p>We do not show both hands with fingers raised. To show them on the <i>Yupana</i>, we need more than one line with circles. We will show more details about this on another page.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a62b520a-f443-4d83-ab51-b99c9a46dab8",
                     "text": "Prem, Dhavit",
                     "page": "pp. 355",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2018",
                        "month": "04",
                        "day": "01"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "f5b74f18-0bc4-46d2-8a5c-64dd80b74dd1",
                     "text": "Poma de Ayala, Felipe Guaman",
                     "page": "pp. 361 - 362",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1615",
                        "month": "02",
                        "day": "13"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "48dd9187-7ecc-4f54-a387-d9ecc2497de3",
                     "text": "W3school",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "01",
                        "day": "27"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2018",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "1615",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_mesoamerica",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>Counting with shapes</h3>The <i>Yupana</i> was not the only way Indigenous people used shapes to count. In another method, circles show the fingertips, and rectangles show all the fingers together (<htcite0> ). To show all fingers down, they used an empty shell. Let's see how they showed our fingers:</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "d1b7daa6-a743-4a66-9cfe-ff4286868f35",
                     "text": "Hernández Mendoza, Aurelio & Pérez López, Felipa",
                     "page": "pp. 251",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2013",
                        "month": "04",
                        "day": "26"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2013",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<table class=\"tawapukllay\" id=\"tblHandsCounting\"><tr><th>Left Hand</th><th>Right hand</th><th>Indigenous couting</th></tr> <tr id=\"tr0\"></tr> <tr id=\"tr1\"></tr> <tr id=\"tr2\"></tr> <tr id=\"tr3\"></tr> <tr id=\"tr4\"></tr> <tr id=\"tr5\"></tr> <tr id=\"tr6\"></tr> <tr id=\"tr7\"></tr> <tr id=\"tr8\"></tr> <tr id=\"tr9\"></tr> <tr id=\"tr10\"></tr>  </table>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 3</b>: Representation of fingers counting with Indigenous methods. Source code available on the project's GitHub (<htcite0>)</p><p>Basic shapes were not the only way to show fingers and counting among Indigenous peoples (<htcite1> ; <htcite2>). But for now, we will focus on these shapes.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "240800f3-ede4-4deb-9d4e-915a3a03fa68",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Código-fonte",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ad11b3b2-8cdf-4d71-9cb7-2ac241850945",
                     "text": "Casares Contreras, Orlando & Cetina, José & Cabrera, Venus Sarahí",
                     "page": "pp. 31 (ResearchGate)",
                     "date_time": {
